\begin{abstract}
Hybrid recurrent architectures that interleave attention layers with linear recurrence are increasingly standard, yet how attention affects per-example behavior across different recurrent backbones remains poorly quantified. We conduct a systematic analysis using the controlled model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}: three architectures (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2) at five attention ratios and two scales (340M and 1.3B parameters), evaluated on 10 benchmarks with 5{,}153 per-example comparisons on LAMBADA. Of the 10 benchmarks, only LAMBADA exhibits cross-architecture convergence ($\rho = -1.0$, BCa 95\% CI $[-1.0, -0.7]$; cross-architecture standard deviation drops 79\%). The Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .100$ does not survive strict correction; the confidence interval serves as primary evidence. This convergence is complete at 1.3B but partial and non-monotonic at 340M; sufficient scale appears necessary for full equalization. Despite aggregate convergence, per-example helped and hurt sets are nearly disjoint across architectures (Jaccard similarity $< 0.10$), and this overlap saturates at minimal (4\%) attention. Aggregate performance equalizes progressively, while per-example specificity is fixed from the earliest attention allocation. These results quantify underspecification in hybrid architecture design: aggregate equivalence does not imply per-example behavioral interchangeability.
\end{abstract}

